#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   9847214
#=GF RT   The Ribonuclease P Database.
#=GF RA   Brown JW
#=GF RL   Nucleic Acids Res 1999;27:314.
#=GF CC   Ribonuclease P (RNase P) is a ubiquitous endoribonuclease, found in
#=GF CC   archaea, bacteria and eukarya as well as chloroplasts and mitochondria.
#=GF CC   Its best characterised activity is the generation of mature 5'-ends of
#=GF CC   tRNAs by cleaving the 5'-leader elements of precursor-tRNAs. Cellular
#=GF CC   RNase Ps are ribonucleoproteins. RNA from bacterial RNase Ps retains its
#=GF CC   catalytic activity in the absence of the protein subunit, i.e. it is a
#=GF CC   ribozyme. Isolated eukaryotic and archaeal RNase P RNA has not been shown
#=GF CC   to retain its catalytic function, but is still essential for the catalytic
#=GF CC   activity of the holoenzyme. Although the archaeal and eukaryotic
#=GF CC   holoenzyme s have a much greater protein content than the bacterial ones,
#=GF CC   the RNA cores from all the three lineages are homologous -- helices
#=GF CC   corresponding to P1, P2, P3, P4, and P10/11 are common to all cellular
#=GF CC   RNase P RNAs. Yet, there is considerable sequence variation, particularly
#=GF CC   among the eukaryotic RNAs.
#=GF WK   Ribonuclease_P
#=GF SQ   114


