#=GF DR   SO; 0000587; group_I_intron;
#=GF DR   GO; 0000372; Group I intron splicing;
#=GF RN   [1]
#=GF RM   2197983
#=GF RT   Self-splicing of group I introns.
#=GF RA   Cech TR
#=GF RL   Annu Rev Biochem 1990;59:543-568.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   8781224
#=GF RT   Crystal structure of a group I ribozyme domain: principles of RNA packing.
#=GF RA   Cate JH, Gooding AR, Podell E, Zhou K, Golden BL, Kundrot CE, Cech TR,
#=GF RA   Doudna JA
#=GF RL   Science 1996;273:1678-1685.
#=GF CC   Group I catalytic introns are large self-splicing ribozymes. They catalyse
#=GF CC   their own excision from mRNA, tRNA and rRNA precursors in a wide range of
#=GF CC   organisms. The core secondary structure consists of 9 paired regions
#=GF CC   (P1-P9). These fold to essentially two domains - the P4-P6 domain (formed
#=GF CC   from the stacking of P5, P4, P6 and P6a helices) and the P3-P9 domain
#=GF CC   (formed from the P8, P3, P7 and P9 helices). The secondary structure
#=GF CC   mark-up for this family represents only this conserved core. Group I
#=GF CC   catalytic introns often have long ORFs inserted in loop regions. The
#=GF CC   window size for this search is chosen to minimise search time and catch
#=GF CC   the small examples. A larger window size will find more matches.
#=GF WK   Group_I_catalytic_intron
#=GF SQ   12


