#=GF AC   RF00031
#=GF ID   SECIS_1
#=GF PI   SECIS
#=GF DE   Selenocysteine insertion sequence 1
#=GF AU   Griffiths-Jones SR; 0000-0001-6043-807X
#=GF SE   Gautheret D, PMID:12458087
#=GF SS   Published; PMID:12458087
#=GF GA   35.00
#=GF TC   35.00
#=GF NC   34.90
#=GF TP   Cis-reg;
#=GF BM   cmbuild -F CM SEED
#=GF CB   cmcalibrate --mpi CM
#=GF SM   cmsearch --cpu 4 --verbose --nohmmonly -E 1000 -Z 549862.597050 CM SEQDB
#=GF DR   SO; 1001274; SECIS_element;
#=GF DR   GO; 0001514; selenocysteine incorporation;
#=GF RN   [1]
#=GF RM   8634917
#=GF RT   A novel RNA structural motif in the selenocysteine insertion element of
#=GF RT   eukaryotic selenoprotein mRNAs.
#=GF RA   Walczak R, Westhof E, Carbon P, Krol A
#=GF RL   RNA 1996;2:367-379.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   12458087
#=GF RT   A survey of metazoan selenocysteine insertion sequences.
#=GF RA   Lambert A, Lescure A, Gautheret D
#=GF RL   Biochimie 2002;84:953-959.
#=GF CC   The incorporation of selenocysteine into a protein sequence is directed by
#=GF CC   an in-frame UGA codon (usually a stop codon) within the coding region of
#=GF CC   the mRNA. Selenoprotein mRNAs contain a conserved secondary structure in
#=GF CC   the 3' UTR that is required for the distinction of UGA stop from UGA
#=GF CC   selenocysteine. The selenocysteine insertion sequence (SECIS) is around 60
#=GF CC   nt in length and adopts a hairpin structure which is sufficiently
#=GF CC   well-defined and conserved to act as a computational screen for
#=GF CC   selenoprotein genes [2].
#=GF WK   SECIS_element
#=GF SQ   61


