#=GF RT   Insertion sequence IS10 anti-sense pairing initiates by an interaction
#=GF RT   between the 5' end of the target RNA and a loop in the anti-sense RNA.
#=GF RA   Kittle JD, Simons RW, Lee J, Kleckner N
#=GF RL   J Mol Biol 1989;210:561-572.
#=GF CC   Transposition of insertion sequence IS10 is regulated by an anti-sense RNA
#=GF CC   which inhibits transposase expression when IS10 is present in multiple
#=GF CC   copies per cell. The anti-sense RNA (RNA-OUT) consists of a stem domain
#=GF CC   topped by a flexibly paired loop; the 5' end of the target molecule,
#=GF CC   RNA-IN, is complementary to the top of the loop, and complementarity
#=GF CC   extends for 35 base-pairs down one side of RNA-OUT [1]. This bacterial RNA
#=GF CC   is also found in the genomic sequence of eukaryotic organisms. This is
#=GF CC   probably due to vector contamination.
