#=GF AC   RF00044
#=GF ID   Phage_pRNA
#=GF DE   Bacteriophage pRNA
#=GF AU   Finn RD; 0000-0001-8626-2148
#=GF SE   Finn RD
#=GF SS   Predicted; ILM
#=GF GA   40.00
#=GF TC   61.90
#=GF NC   35.90
#=GF TP   Gene;
#=GF BM   cmbuild -F CM SEED
#=GF CB   cmcalibrate --mpi CM
#=GF SM   cmsearch --cpu 4 --verbose --nohmmonly -E 1000 -Z 549862.597050 CM SEQDB
#=GF DR   SO; 0000655; ncRNA;
#=GF RN   [1]
#=GF RM   12206459
#=GF RT   Structure and function of phi29 hexameric RNA that drives the viral DNA
#=GF RT   packaging motor: review.
#=GF RA   Guo P
#=GF RL   Prog Nucleic Acid Res Mol Biol 2002;72:415-472.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   11562164
#=GF RT   Cryoelectron-microscopy image reconstruction of symmetry mismatches in
#=GF RT   bacteriophage phi29.
#=GF RA   Morais MC, Tao Y, Olson NH, Grimes S, Jardine PJ, Anderson DL, Baker TS,
#=GF RA   Rossmann MG
#=GF RL   J Struct Biol 2001;135:38-46.
#=GF RN   [3]
#=GF RM   11130079
#=GF RT   Structure of the bacteriophage phi29 DNA packaging motor.
#=GF RA   Simpson AA, Tao Y, Leiman PG, Badasso MO, He Y, Jardine PJ, Olson NH,
#=GF RA   Morais MC, Grimes S, Anderson DL, Baker TS, Rossmann MG
#=GF RL   Nature 2000;408:745-750.
#=GF RN   [4]
#=GF RM   10957642
#=GF RT   Purification, crystallization and initial X-ray analysis of the head-tail
#=GF RT   connector of bacteriophage phi29.
#=GF RA   Badasso MO, Leiman PG, Tao Y, He Y, Ohlendorf DH, Rossmann MG, Anderson D
#=GF RL   Acta Crystallogr D Biol Crystallogr 2000;56:1187-1190.
#=GF RN   [5]
#=GF RM   3107124
#=GF RT   A small viral RNA is required for in vitro packaging of bacteriophage phi
#=GF RT   29 DNA.
#=GF RA   Guo PX, Erickson S, Anderson D
#=GF RL   Science 1987;236:690-694.
#=GF RN   [6]
#=GF RM   2125049
#=GF RT   Phylogenetic analysis and secondary structure of the Bacillus subtilis
#=GF RT   bacteriophage RNA required for DNA packaging.
#=GF RA   Bailey S, Wichitwechkarn J, Johnson D, Reilly BE, Anderson DL, Bodley JW
#=GF RL   J Biol Chem 1990;265:22365-22370.
#=GF CC   During replication of linear dsDNA viruses, the viral genome is packaged
#=GF CC   into the pre-formed viral procapsid. The packaging of DNA into the
#=GF CC   procapsid requires a molecular motor which using ATP as energy to
#=GF CC   accomplish the energetically unfavourable motion. In some bacteriophage,
#=GF CC   an RNA (pRNA) molecule is a vital component of this motor [1]. Structural
#=GF CC   analyses of the packaging motor have demonstrated that the pRNA molecule
#=GF CC   has fivefold symmetry when attached to the prohead [2-4]. The pRNA is
#=GF CC   thought to be bound by the capsid connector protein (PFAM:PF05352) [4].
#=GF CC   Only the first 120 bases of the pRNA are essential for packing the viral
#=GF CC   DNA [5,6]. The pRNA is proposed to be comprised of two domains, one
#=GF CC   corresponding to the first 120 bases and the second to the remaining 50
#=GF CC   bases [6]. Nuclear cleavage occurs in the single strand region linking
#=GF CC   these two domains [6].
#=GF WK   Bacteriophage_pRNA
#=GF SQ   3


